<h2>Content pillars: your cheat code for never running out of ideas</h2>

<p>The blank screen is the enemy. And the best weapon against it is a set of content pillars. These are three to five recurring themes that you rotate between so you always know what type of post to create next.</p>

<p>Here is a pillar framework that works for almost any small business:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Educational.</strong> Teach your audience something useful. Quick tips, how-to advice, common mistakes, myth-busting. This positions you as the expert and gives people a reason to follow you beyond just liking your products.</li>
  <li><strong>Behind the scenes.</strong> Show the human side of your business. Your workspace, your process, your team, the funny moments. People connect with people, not logos. Let them see the real version of your business.</li>
  <li><strong>Social proof.</strong> Reviews, testimonials, client results, before and after photos, case studies. Nothing builds trust like showing that real people have worked with you and loved the experience. This pairs well with your <a href="/blog/get-more-google-reviews">Google review strategy</a> too.</li>
  <li><strong>Promotional.</strong> Your offers, services, availability, new launches, seasonal specials. Keep this to roughly 20 percent of your content. If every post is a sales pitch, people tune out fast.</li>
  <li><strong>Community.</strong> Shout-outs to other local businesses, reposts from customers, community events, industry news. This builds goodwill and makes your feed feel like a conversation, not a billboard.</li>
</ul>

<p>When it is time to batch, you simply cycle through your pillars. Monday is educational. Wednesday is behind the scenes. Friday is social proof. You are never staring at a blank screen wondering what to post because the category is already decided. Your job is just to fill in the details.</p>

<p>The pillars also keep your feed balanced. Without them, most business owners default to either posting promotional content (which turns people off) or posting random thoughts (which does not build towards anything). Pillars give you structure without making you feel boxed in.</p>

