<h2>The minimum viable posting schedule</h2>

<p>Let go of the idea that you need to post every day. You do not. For a small business, three posts per week is plenty if those posts are consistent, on-brand, and backed by real engagement.</p>

<p>Here is what "enough" looks like:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>3 feed posts per week.</strong> One educational, one social proof or behind the scenes, one community or promotional. Rotate through your pillars and keep the mix varied.</li>
  <li><strong>A few Stories per week.</strong> These do not need to be planned. Grab your phone, share something real, and let it disappear after 24 hours. Stories keep you visible between feed posts and reward spontaneity.</li>
  <li><strong>10 to 15 minutes of daily engagement.</strong> Reply to every comment and DM. Leave a few comments on other accounts. This is the glue that holds everything together.</li>
</ul>

<p>That is it. Three posts, a handful of Stories, and a quarter-hour of interaction each day. During your weekly batch session, you are creating those three posts in about 30 to 45 minutes. The rest is just showing up briefly each day to respond and connect.</p>

<p>The businesses that win on social media are not the ones posting the most. They are the ones posting consistently and engaging genuinely. A competitor who posts every day but never responds to comments will lose to a business that posts three times a week and replies to everyone. Consistency and connection beat volume every time.</p>

<blockquote><p>Three great posts per week with real engagement will outperform seven mediocre posts with no interaction. Every time.</p></blockquote>

